.TH CBMAP_INSERT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_insert \- insert a key-value pair (type-inferred)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_insert(hm_name, key, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for cbmap_insert_elem(). It builds the cmap_pair for
the key and the cmap_pair for the value from the expressions of the
caller. If the key is already in the map, the macro updates the value
and reports nothing. The macro calls ccol_fatal_err() only on a real
failure, that is ccol_not_enough_memory or ccol_container_full. The map
balances the tree again after the insert.

key and val go through the declared key type and value type of the
map, as NOTES describes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  BST map to insert into
key      Key to insert
val      Value for that key
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
key and val go through the DECLARED key type and value type of the
tree. The macro makes a private copy of each argument with that type,
and the copy is a plain C assignment, so the value is CONVERTED. It
is never reinterpreted byte for byte. A float that goes into a map of
int becomes 1 for 1.5f, in the same way as "int x = 1.5f;". It does
not become 1069547520. An argument whose type has no implicit
conversion to the declared type is a compile error at the call site.
cvec_push(3) follows the same rule.

Because the macro copies rather than takes an address, an lvalue, an
rvalue, a literal and a computed expression are all accepted.

A declared key or value type that is a CHARACTER POINTER is the one
exception, and it converts nothing. char *, signed char * and
unsigned char * are one single type to this library: all three
classify as ccol_string, a NUL-terminated string. The macro measures
it with strlen, and the tree compares the bytes of the string and
never the pointer type. A map keyed on unsigned char * therefore
stays reachable through a char * that addresses the same string. An
array key, such as a char buf[64] or a string literal, decays to a
pointer in the same way as it does in any other assignment.

A macro cannot know the length of a buffer that a pointer addresses,
so a character pointer is never a binary key. Use one of two forms
for a binary key. A key of a fixed length, such as a digest, can be
a struct that wraps an array, such as
struct { unsigned char b[32]; }; the tree orders by all of its
bytes, zero bytes included. A key whose length is known only at run
time goes through the raw cbmap_insert_elem(3) layer, with that
length in cmap_pair.size.

A NULL character pointer is not a string. A NULL key or value of a
character-pointer type stops the program with ccol_invalid_args.

The macro handles a value type and a string type correctly
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3),
.BR cbmap_get (3),
.BR cbmap_remove (3)
